{"type":"message","id":"f4f4abfc","parentId":"8683e204","timestamp":"2026-10-06T05:56:12.858Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent. Key finding: fnhotkeyutility.exe contains a mixed-mode (C++/CLI) assembly referencing `BlurSwitchCS` namespace and `BlurSwitchCLR.BlurSwitchCLR.Switch` method (`?Switch@BlurSwitchCLR@1@$$FSAHXZ`). So fnhotkey loads BlurSwitchCS via CLR and calls a static `Switch()` in class `BlurSwitchCLR` (mangled: `__m2mep@?Switch@BlurSwitchCLR@1@$$FSAHXZ`). And log string \"e_Event_CAMERA_BG_BLUR BlurSwitchCLR Switch retBlur=%d\". So BlurSwitchCLR is likely a mixed-mode wrapper class in fnhotkey's C++/CLI, calling BlurSwitchCS.ToggleBlurSwitch.\n\nWait — actually the strings \"BlurSwitchCS\" and \"ToggleBlurSwitch\" appear in fnhotkey's #Strings too, and \"BlurSwitchCLR.BlurSwitchCLR.Switch\". So fnhotkey.exe contains the CLR metadata referencing BlurSwitchCS assembly and defining managed class BlurSwitchCLR with method Switch. Hmm — could be fnhotkey is a C++/CLI DLL embedding. The `__m2mep@?Switch@BlurSwitchCLR@...` is a native thunk name for managed method `BlurSwitchCLR.BlurSwitchCLR.Switch()`. Yes, C++/CLI.\n\nSo: FnHotkeyUtility (C++/CLI, mixed-mode) handles `e_Event_CAMERA_BG_BLUR` and calls `BlurSwitchCLR.Switch()` which uses BlurSwitchCS class → AppService.\n\nAnd \"CameraPrivacy SwitchCameraStatus\" in fnhotkey is a separate mechanism: it CoCreates a COM object (moniker), enumerates cameras (`nIndexCamera`), calls `pCameraControl[i]->Set` — this is Windows Camera privacy setting via the HKCU/registry or the `Windows.Media.Devices`? Actually it appears to be the \"Camera privacy\" registry Control panel? The COM moniker for camera privacy... Possibly related to the camera on/off toggle in Windows Settings (DisableCamera registry under CapabilityAccessManager). That's about *enabling/disabling* the camera globally in Windows, not the switch state.\n\nNow, question 4: does the camera switch *state*