ionKeys`; starts/stops worker, mic-mute LED (`mmstate`/`LSK_SetFeature`), WMI method `SetFeature` on `ROOT\\\\WMI` | launches worker only |\\n| `FnHotkeyUtility.exe` (767 KB, C++/CLI mixed-mode) | The event worker/UI: subscribes `ROOT\\\\WMI` `LENOVO_UTILITY_EVENT` (`CMoniterWmiUtilityEvent`), `MsgLogic::g_HandleEvent` dispatch, OSD window `Lenovo_Utility_Window` | **owns** camera switch event (CAMERA_SWITCH_ON/OFF) and BG-blur trigger |\\n| `BlurSwitchCS.dll` (15.4 KB, .NET 4.6.1) | AppService client: `BlurSwitchCS.ToggleBlurSwitch()` | **toggles camera background blur** |\\n| `ludp.dll` / `ludp.net.dll` | Lenovo User Data Platform telemetry SDK (PDB `e:\\\\hudson\\\\workspace\\\\Ludp.Win.sdk`, SQLite `ludp_event` store, HTTP upload) | none |\\n| `controllib.dll` | Lenovo generic ControlLib (`ctlInitPerAdapter`, `ctlEnumerateDevices`, `ctlSetRuntimePath`; PDB `…\\\\CtlApi\\\\bin\\\\Release\\\\ControlLib.pdb`) | none |\\n| `intelcontrollib.dll` | \\\"Intel Graphics Control Lib Runtime\\\" v1.0.12865 (IGCL: ArcSync, FramePacing, EnduranceGaming) | none |\\n| `atiadlxx.dll` | AMD Display Library (ADL Component 7.21.40.1454) | none (only GPU API names) |\\n| `mmstate.dll` | \\\"Helper dll for mic mute indicator\\\" (Lenovo, 2018) | none (mic only) |\\n| `lenovo.assemblyvalidation.native.dll` | native signature/validation helper | none |\\n| `msvcp140/vcruntime140(_1).dll` | MSVC runtime | none |\\n\\n## 3. End-to-end data flow (INF → service → worker → UWP AppService → OSD)\\n\\n1. **Firmware**: the physical camera shutter sets EC byte `LSKD` (`20-acpi/dsl/DSDT.dsl:14823,14833`)\\n   and the camera-port gate `CAMS` (`DSDT.dsl:13785`) which drives `HSP3._UPC` (`:6744`) and\\n   `CAMA._STA` (`:6830`).\\n2. **ACPI notify**: firmware issues `Notify (WMIU, 0xD0)` (`DSDT.dsl:14851`); `WMIU._WED`\\n   (`:24676`) converts `LSKD` to a WMI event value.\\n3. **WMI event**: delivered as `ROOT\\\\WMI` event class `LENOVO_UTILITY_EVENT` to\\n   `FnHotkeyUtility.exe` (`CMoniterWmiUtilityEvent`, strings16-fnhotkeyutility.exe.txt:670-674;\\n   `strings-fnhotkeyutility.exe.txt` shows `SELECT * FROM LENOVO_UTILITY_EVENT`).\\n4. **Worker decode**: `MsgLogic::g_HandleEvent` (`…:900`) reads the event payload and derives the\\n   camera state from a bit: `MsgLogic g_HandleEvent SwitchCameraStatus in ....` (`:902`),\\n   `m_bExistRFDevice&8 pInfoData->Data[0] %d` (`:904-905`), `camera unkown status!` (`:906`),\\n   producing `e_Event_CAMERA_SWITCH_ON`/`e_Event_CAMERA_SWITCH_OFF` (`:1000-1001`).\\n   The Windows camera-privacy setting is then **written** through a COM camera-control object:\\n   `CameraPrivacy SwitchCameraStatus CoCreateInstance … pMoniker[nIndexCamera]->BindToObject …\\n   pCameraControl[i]->Set` (`:410-414`).\\n5. **Background blur (separate feature)**: event `e_Event_CAMERA_BG_BLUR` (`:1027`) →\\n   `BlurSwitchCLR.Switch()` (managed method inside fnhotkey.exe;\\n   `strings-fnhotkeyutility.exe.txt:3074` `BlurSwitchCLR.BlurSwitchCLR.Switch`, log\\n   `e_Event_CAMERA_BG_BLUR BlurSwitchCLR Switch retBlur=%d` `:1060`) →\\n   `BlurSwitchCS.dll` `ToggleBlurSwitch()` → UWP AppService.\\n   If it failed/absent, fnhotkey falls back to launching `lenovo-smartappearance:backgroundsettingpage`\\n   or the Store URL `ms-windows-store://pdp/?productid=9NRLFDZ54PZB` (`:1081-1085`).\\n6. **UWP side**: package `E0469640.SmartAppearance_5grkq8ppsgwt4`, AppService\\n   `Lenovo.CameraControlBackgroundService` (`strings16-blurswitchcs.dll.txt:2-3`) applies the blur\\n   and returns `Result = BlurOn | BlurOff | BlurError`.\\n7. **OSD**: drawn by FnHotkeyUtility's own window; camera OSD gate is `CAMERA_BIT7` in the hotkey\\n   config plus `SYSTEM\\\\CurrentControlSet\\\\Services\\\\LenovoFnAndFunctionKeys\\\\ControlShowOSD`\\n   (`:892,901,908`).\\n\\n## 4. Kernel-mode driver: REFUTED\\n\\n- `glob **/*.sys` over the whole workspace → **no files**.\\n- INF has no `ServiceBinary … .sys` entry; its only service is the user exe (`inf:73-78`).\\n- The `.cat` member list (§1) contains the 16 files above and no `.sys`.\\n- fnhotkey references `EMDriver CKernelLib` / `DeviceIoControl IOCTL_ENERGYDRV_*`\\n  (`strings16-fnhotkeyutility.exe.txt:438-462`) i.e. it *talks to* a kernel driver named\\n  `EMDriver`, but that driver is **not shipped in this package** (it is a separate Lenovo\\n  energy-management driver installed by other packages). [INFERENCE] It is absent here.\\n\\n## 5. Questions 1–4 (brief)\\n\\n**Q1 — AppService name / package family name.**\\nAppService = `Lenovo.CameraControlBackgroundService`; package family = `E0469640.SmartAppearance_5grkq8ppsgwt4`\\n(`strings16-blurswitchcs.dll.txt:2-3`; also `strings16-fnhotkeyutility.exe.txt:1081`).\\n\\u26a0 Correction to STATE.md: the DLL does **not** connect to\\n`E0469640.LenovoUtility_5grkq8ppsgwt4!LenovoUtility`. That AUMID appears only in\\n`FnHotkeyUtility.exe` (`strings16-fnhotkeyutility.exe.txt:854`) and only as\\n`ActivateApplication()` for the superkey (launch the LenovoUtility app), not as an AppService.\\n\\n**Q2 — exact request keys/values and status codes.**\\nRequest `ValueSet`: `\\\"Key\\\"` = `\\\"ToggleBlurSwitch\\\"`. Response `ValueSet`: `\\\"Result\\\"` ∈\\n`{ \\\"BlurOn\\\", \\\"BlurOff\\\", \\\"BlurError\\\" }`. Status constants (return `int`):\\n`STATUS_SET_BLUR_OFF_OK, STATUS_SET_BLUR_ON_OK, STATUS_EXCEPTION, STATUS_SVR_REPOND_ERR [sic],\\nSTATUS_SET_FEATURE_ERR, STATUS_RETURN_ERR, STATUS_CONNECT_SVR_ERR, STATUS_SET_TIMEOUT`\\n(`strings-blurswitchcs.dll.txt:9-16`; `strings16-blurswitchcs.dll.txt:4-6`).\\nNumeric values are not present in metadata (see §B caveat).\\n\\n**Q3 — support DLLs / package identities.**\\n`ludp.dll`, `ludp.net.dll`, `controllib.dll`, `intelcontrollib.dll`, `mmstate.dll` contain **no**\\ncamera/privacy/blur/LVF/SmartAppearance/AppService strings at all (grep over\\n`70-evidence/strings*(-…).txt`). `atiadlxx.dll` matches only GPU-API symbol names\\n`ADL2_ApplicationProfiles_DriverAreaPrivacy_Get`, `ADL2_CVML_Camera_DMFT_Set`\\n(unrelated to the camera privacy switch).\\n- `E0469640.LenovoUtility_5grkq8ppsgwt4!LenovoUtility` → only fnhotkey:854; activates the\\n  LenovoUtility UWP app for the superkey.\\n- `E046963F.LenovoCompanion_k1h2ywk1493x8` → fnhotkey:832; Lenovo Companion/Vantage/PcManager launch.\\n  Also `E046963F.AIMeetingManager_k1h2ywk1493x8` (:625).\\n- The camera/blur feature is **owned by the `E0469640.SmartAppearance_5grkq8ppsgwt4` UWP app**\\n  (AppService `Lenovo.CameraControlBackgroundService`), invoked from `BlurSwitchCS.dll` which is\\n  loaded by `FnHotkeyUtility.exe`. FnHotkeyUtility also has its own camera-privacy path and the\\n  camera-switch event handling.\\n\\n**Q4 — is the camera switch STATE ever read, or only blur toggled?**\\n**No component in this package reads the hardware switch position as a value.** Evidence:\\n- `BlurSwitchCS.dll` exposes only `OpenAsync`, `SendMessageAsync`, `ToggleBlurSwitch` — there is no\\n  getter/query method; it sends a toggle command and returns a *status code*.\\n- The switch status in fnhotkey is derived **from the WMI event payload**, not from a device query:\\n  `MsgLogic g_HandleEvent SwitchCameraStatus in ....` (:902),\\n  `m_bExistRFDevice&8 pInfoData->Data[0] %d` (:904-905), `camera unkown status!` (:906).\\n- The `CameraPrivacy …` path calls `pCameraControl[i]->Set` (:413) — a **write** of the Windows\\n  camera-privacy setting, not a read of hardware state.\\n- [INFERENCE] The only component that could read the authoritative hardware state is the\\n  SmartAppearance UWP app (owner of the AppService) — it is **not** in this package; the Windows\\n  side never exposes a queryable \\\"switch\\\" value other than the transient event bit.\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## §B  DELIVERABLE 2 — `10-extracted/analysis/blurswitchcs.decompiled.cs`\\n\\n> Write the block below verbatim to `/home/user/Downloads/lenovo-camera-switch-re/10-extracted/analysis/blurswitchcs.decompiled.cs`.\\n> Tooling caveat: this agent has no shell/write t